Scoring Rubric
All 100 marks, published before registration opens.

Published in full so you can read exactly how you will be scored before you decide to enter.

The model
#	Category	Marks	Source
1	Functionality	25	Judged
2	Early submission	10	Automatic
3	Problem difficulty	15	Automatic
4	Technical execution	15	Judged
5	UI / UX	15	Judged
6	Demo and documentation	20	Judged
Total	100	
Scored per team across both problems. Categories 1, 4, 5 and 6 are scored once per problem and averaged.

The two automatic categories are 25 of 100 marks. Both are tied to work that actually functions. Difficulty and speed are multipliers on real work, not substitutes for it.
1. Functionality, 25 marks
The heaviest category. Does the MVP solve the stated problem. Every problem lists 4 required MVP bullets. These are pass or fail and a judge checks each in under a minute.

Band	Marks	Descriptor
Exceptional	23 to 25	Every MVP bullet works. Edge cases handled without being asked. The build shows the team understood the real pain in the scenario, not just the bullet list.
Strong	18 to 22	Every MVP bullet works. Happy path solid, edges thin.
Adequate	13 to 17	Most bullets work. One missing or partial. Demoable.
Weak	7 to 12	Runs, but half the requirements are missing or the core flow breaks under real input.
Minimal	1 to 6	Runs, but does not meaningfully address the problem.
Non-functional	0	Does not run.
2. Early submission, 10 marks
1.25 marks for every complete 30 minutes remaining when you stop working.

How the time is measured
From your last commit across both repositories, not from a form. Keep committing and your judged score keeps improving while your speed bonus falls. That trade-off is the actual game, it is identical for everyone, and it cannot be gamed by submitting a shell early and quietly rebuilding afterwards.
Last commit at	Remaining	Blocks	Bonus
3h 30m	30m	1	1.25
3h 00m	1h 00m	2	2.50
2h 30m	1h 30m	3	3.75
2h 00m	2h 00m	4	5.00
1h 30m	2h 30m	5	6.25
1h 00m	3h 00m	6	7.50
0h 30m	3h 30m	7	8.75, the practical maximum
The category is worth 10, but 8.75 is the highest score anyone can reach. The eighth block would require a last commit within the first 20 minutes of the window, before any real work exists. Nobody is losing marks another team can win: the ceiling is the same for every entrant.
Partial blocks do not count: a last commit at 2h 15m gives 3 blocks (3.75 marks), not 3.5 blocks.

Prayer credit: every team's clock is reduced by a flat 20 minutes automatically, applied identically whether a team prays or not. Prayer breaks themselves are taken on request during the event, with no requirement to keep the camera on; the credit is separate and does not depend on asking for one.

The gate
The bonus is awarded only if at least 3 of the 4 required MVP bullets are verified working on each of your two problems. Countable, checkable, and appealable against evidence rather than against a judge's feel for a band. Below that threshold the bonus is 0 no matter how early you stopped.
Why the gate exists
Without it, 25 of 100 marks are handed out before a judge looks at anything, and one strategy dominates: pick two Hard problems, ship broken skeletons in the first hour, and beat teams that spent four hours building working software. The gate is what stops that. It is deliberately not a high bar, three working bullets is well short of a good submission, but it is a real one.

3. Problem difficulty, 15 marks
Tier	Full credit
Easy	5.0
Medium	6.5
Hard	7.5
Two problems, so the ceiling is 15.0 (two Hard).

Credit is scaled by what you delivered
Credit = tier value x (required MVP bullets working / total required bullets for that problem).
Example	Calculation	Credit
Hard, all 4 bullets working	7.5 x 1.00	7.5
Hard, 3 of 4 working	7.5 x 0.75	5.6
Easy, all 4 working	5.0 x 1.00	5.0
Hard, does not run	7.5 x 0	0
Picking Hard and shipping nothing is worth less than picking Easy and shipping something that works. Without this scaling, difficulty credit is a flat 15 for every competent team: it separates nobody while inflating every total, and it makes picking Hard free money.

4. Technical execution, 15 marks
Band	Marks	Descriptor
Exceptional	14 to 15	Clean data model reflecting the domain. Sensible separation of concerns. Deployed properly. Real edge case handling.
Strong	11 to 13	Reasonable structure, readable code, clean deployment.
Adequate	7 to 10	Works, but structure is ad hoc and state is handled loosely.
Weak	3 to 6	Copy-paste heavy, no discernible model, barely deployed.
Minimal	0 to 2	Unrunnable outside the authors' machines.
AI tool use is not penalised. A well-structured AI-assisted build scores higher than a badly structured hand-written one. We score the artifact and the judgement behind it. Commit history is an integrity signal, not a scoring dimension.
5. UI / UX, 15 marks
Band	Marks	Descriptor
Exceptional	13 to 15	A stranger can use it without instructions. Layout serves the task. Empty, loading and error states handled.
Strong	10 to 12	Clean and usable, one or two rough states.
Adequate	7 to 9	Functional and plain. Default components, no thought applied.
Weak	3 to 6	Confusing layout, unlabelled controls, broken on narrow screens.
Minimal	0 to 2	Unstyled output.
Not scored: decoration for its own sake. A plain clear interface beats a decorated confusing one.

6. Demo and documentation, 20 marks
Component	Marks
Demo video, no less than 60 seconds, shows it working, covers the MVP bullets	8
README, what it does, how to run it, what is mocked, what is next	6
LICENSES.md, complete and accurate	4
Submission completeness, repo, live URL, picks declared, fields correct	2
The video scores above 4 only if a judge can verify claimed MVP bullets in the recording, and those bullets are then confirmed live. A polished video of a product that does not do what it shows scores nothing.
7. The archive, what you are judged against
At 22:00 on event day, before anything is judged:

Every repository's write access is revoked and its commit and push history is captured.
Every live URL receives an automated HTTP check and a full-page screenshot, archived.
Judging happens a week later against that archive. A free-tier container that sleeps on 3 September cannot cost you marks for work that was live and working at 22:00 on 30 August.

8. Tiebreaks
Higher Functionality score.
Higher Functionality on the team's better-scoring problem.
Earlier last commit.
Higher Technical execution.
Event lead's decision, recorded with reasons.
9. How judging runs
Stage	What happens
Auto-score	Difficulty and bonus computed from the archive. No human input.
Screen	Does the URL load, does it address the declared problem. Pass or fail, with a one-line written reason for every fail, confirmed by the Judging Lead before it is actioned.
Deep judge	Functionality, Technical execution, UI/UX, Demo scored against the bands above.
Calibration	Before live scoring, all judges independently score the same two anchor submissions and reconcile.
Double-scoring	Every submission within 5 marks of a prize boundary is scored by two judges and averaged.
Sign-off	Final table, tiebreaks, appeals.
Does the submitted code address the problem it was declared under? If not, that problem's difficulty credit is 0, not just its Functionality score.

10. Individual observation, internship selection
Separate from the 100 marks, and it decides up to three internship offers. Proctors score every participant 1 to 5 on six signals, recorded during the event at the 19:30, 20:30 and 21:30 marks, not reconstructed afterwards.

Signal	What it looks like
Decomposition	Planned before typing
Communication	Explains thinking clearly, asks good questions
Debugging under pressure	Stays methodical when something breaks at hour three
Ownership	Takes a piece and finishes it
Tool judgement	Directs AI tools and checks their output rather than pasting blindly
Composure	Behaviour when things go wrong, and toward teammates
A strong individual on a team that placed nowhere is fully eligible. The scoreboard tells us which team was fastest. This tells us who we want to work with.
